            <ArticleTitle>Isolated subacute tuberculous spinal epidural abscess of the cervical spine: a brief report of a special case.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>695-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00701-009-0233-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A tuberculous spinal epidural abscess is seen rarely as a late complication of Pott's disease or in immunocompromised patients. Such abscesses in isolation are rare indeed and very uncommon in the developed and developing world. We report a patient with an isolated subacute tuberculous spinal epidural abscess without disc or vertebral involvement and no primary focus or risk factors associated with the development of spinal tuberculosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
